  "slug": "learning-causation-2f3a",
  "summary": "New shrinkage, benchmarking and integration methods target compositionality and confounding as microbiome cohorts and variant-level analyses scale.",
  "synthesis": "Statistical inference for microbiome data is the dominant thread, with attention to compositionality, confounding and calibration. PGL uses Bayesian multinomial logit-normal shrinkage with phylogenetic borrowing for ASV-level feature selection. A benchmark of 71 single-cohort and 63 multi-cohort confounder-aware differential abundance strategies, with xConDA released, evaluates methods by simulation and real data. A high-dimensional structural equation framework integrates large microbiome cohorts with smaller paired microbiome-metabolome data to build microbiome-metabolome-disease pathways. A microbiome-wide PheWAS tests 12.8M gut microbial SNVs against 244 host phenotypes in 10,781 people, yielding 940 associations and a public summary-statistics catalogue. Adjacent work outside microbiome data, ssGSEA3.0's feature-level bootstrap and a 12-method differential composition benchmark in single-cell data, addresses related calibration questions. GutBiomeDB adds a database resource.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-05"
}
